A||N||5||CraigC||Hard Shifting||||5||
Z||000000||CraigC||11-23-2001||10:48 PM||||Just purchased an 84 with the 2.5l cylinder, 100,000 miles. This is from a one time owner, the car came complete with a GM service manual, original window sticker price sheet, articles from 1984 Road & Track and every receipt from the date of purchase for all and any work that was completed. I purchased this car for $500.00 Canadian and it is in awesome condition. I am experiencing some stiff shifting (knew this when I bought it)Mainly between 1st and 2nd gears. This happens wether the car is cold or warm and not all the time. Does it require Clutch fluid change or should I be prepared to spend some bucks, nothing is lost so far! Has been fun to drive so far. Any opinions please feed them my way||||reg||5
Z||000001||842m8||11-24-2001||01:28 AM||||my 84 is the exact same way.... I've gotten use to it but I know my clutch is going because it grinds into reverse. I've bled my clutch and it helped a little but I don't think thats the problem. I'm going to take my tranny apart to see if I can find something, if I happen to fix it I'll let ya know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroRyan||11-24-2001||02:38 AM||||I get the same thing...  if you figure out what it is, let me know - I'd like to fix that before I sell.||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnt671||11-24-2001||08:17 AM||||I had the same problem with my 84.  Even after have the clutch replaced. Bleed the crap out of the clutch and follow V-8 Archies method of bleeding.  I did this, replaced the fluid with Valvoline Synthetic brake fluid and drained the trans out and used Mobil 1.  This took care of almost all my shifting problems.  I have a steel clutch pedal but haven't but it in yet.  Check to see if you clutch pedal is higher than your brake pedal as the original pedals bend.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88CoupeV6||11-24-2001||08:50 AM||||The 4 speed is a little stiff. I would get some white lithium lube and spray both ends of the linkage. Then....<P><BR>I drained the fluid in the trans and added the redline MTL, I feels so nice now. It shifts with less effort and feels<BR>precise.<P>It is the only stuff I use and is better than Synchromesh fluid, It is made by<BR>redline. It is called MTL. It gives a huge difference in shifter feel and protects the trans. & syncros better than most anything on the market. It is purple oil. Look at it at <A HREF="http://www.redlineoil.com/redlineoil/mtlti.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.redlineoil.com/redlineoil/mtlti.htm</A> <P>You can do a search online to find a place that sells it. For best feel and<BR>protection for your trans. I would recommend it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||3
Z||000005||DKOV||11-24-2001||12:28 PM||||I had this problem with two of my previous Fieros...<P>After doing everything noted above AND even performing the pedal swap on one, I found the problem to be much more simple (but not simple to fix)...<P>Turns out that the shifter cables will STRETCH over time.  The extra "length" makes the shifts not slide around smoothly and each shift encounters some resistance.  It is especially noticeable from 1st to 2nd and many times from 2nd to 3rd at higher revs.<P>New cables fixed the problem instantly.  The problem HERE is the effort it takes to replace these two badboys!!!  What a PAIN!<P>In the end, the butter smooth shifting is worth it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
